From: mh2lm clock work <clock@mh2lm>
Subject: [PATCH 0248] feat(rsc): read one resource out of the command DB through a shared query

C3-02 asks for one thing the C3-01 channel does not yet have: a way to turn a
resource name into the metadata the block holds for it, without a second
interpretation of the block.  The 0141 ring proved the channel can map the three
windows, read the header and describe the line; it reads the identity, the DB
header and the TCS word, and it has no query at all.  This patch adds the query
as a separate translation unit, one row in the ring, and one property in the
tree, and it holds the channel's own budget fixed at ten loads while it does it.

The module.  SweetRSCDatabase.h/.cpp is a plain C++ class with no IOKit in it:
lookup() takes a byte count, a name, an auxiliary mask and a read thunk, walks
the chunk descriptors at the top of the header, matches the eight-byte key the
owner builds, applies the owner's per-class admission for the chunk's slave
class, and returns a SweetRSCResource.  Every gate carries the stage it refused
at, so a caller can tell "this block has no such resource" from "this block
could not be read" - which the two failure modes need, because one is a tree
that names a resource the firmware does not ship and the other is a mapping
that is not there.

Parity, and where it stops.  The admission rules are the owner's, in the owner's
order, from SweetRPMh.cpp: the shared chunk gate (slave 3..5, word-aligned entry
table, entry table inside the block, auxiliary base inside the block), then per
class - rail: address high word 4 and a 16-byte slot, auxiliary window inside
the block; ARC: address high word 3, word-aligned address, halfword-aligned
level table, four to thirty-two even bytes, the table and its padding inside the
block, then the levels themselves (first level zero, strictly ascending, a zero
prefix only after the last real level, at least two levels); bandwidth: address
high word 5, word-aligned address, exactly eight auxiliary bytes on a
word-aligned table with both words inside the block, then the unit, the width
and the vcd the owner demands before it votes.  The load order is the owner's
too: two descriptor words, then two key words per entry until the key matches,
then the address and the auxiliary descriptor, then the class's own words.  What
is deliberately *not* copied is the owner's sibling alias check: that reads
fBCMAvailable, the owner's own bookkeeping, not DB content, and a shared module
cannot express it.  It stays where it is.

Two places where this reader is less strict than the draft, both to match the
owner.  First, the shared chunk gate does not require the auxiliary base to be
word aligned; the owner only range-checks it, and a reader that refuses a chunk
the owner resolves is a disagreement in the one direction this card cannot
afford.  The fixture now builds a block whose second chunk has its auxiliary base
two bytes off the running end and requires the rail entry in it to resolve;
putting the alignment test back makes that case fail.  Second, the ARC level
table is only halfword aligned, so the word holding a level is the word below
it, read through `offset & ~3` and cached across the pair - the owner's db16()
does the same, and a load at the halfword address would fault on this mapping.

The channel.  The query is opt-in per boot, because a tree that does not name a
resource gets no row and no load: `sweet,rsc-query` is a four-byte integer
property on the channel node, value 1, and the channel refuses any other length
or value with an ERROR row that names both the value it found and the one it
expects.  With the property present start() reads the identity, the DB header
and then the query, and only after that the TCS word: the query is the one place
it can run, with the block mapped and the geometry known.  The load accounting
is kept apart on purpose.  fReads stays the ten identity loads the 0141 ring
pins, and the query's loads are counted by the lookup itself and printed in the
row and in the summary separately, so "ten loads" keeps meaning what C3-01
established and the query's cost is visible instead of folded in.  One load site:
the query's thunk goes through the same readWindow() the rest of the channel
uses, so the translation unit still has exactly one volatile read in it.

The rows.  Success is one INFO row with the resource's class, address, entry,
auxiliary address and length, unit, width and vcd, its match count and the loads
it took.  Failure is one WARN naming the stage, the load count and
detail=query-refused - a warning on purpose: a boot where the tree names a
resource the block does not have is a boot this binary should make loud, and one
that does not name a resource prints nothing at all.  Neither path can write:
the class has no store helper, and the channel's submit() is still
kIOReturnNotReady.

The test-side comparison.  C3-02's own criterion is that the new reader agrees
with the old one about a resource this platform is known to use, so the device
check compares the db-query row against the display owner's own INFO row for
CN0, which this boot prints earlier: name, address, unit, width and vcd, field
by field.  enable-mask is excluded because it is caller policy (the mask the
channel passes in), not DB content.  The auditor grew --c3-02 for that ring and
refuses to judge a query ring as C3-01 or a channel ring as C3-02, because the
row shape and the summary row have to agree with the binary that printed them.

Host evidence.  tools/tests/test-mh2lm-rsc-database.py compiles the shipped
module against a fixture that builds the block from a description and parses it
back, with a two-sided case for every gate and the load count pinned (CN0 takes
26 loads out of the fixture block, the channel's own fixture 18, a missing name
14).  test-mh2lm-rsc-channel.py gained the four property cases and a query ring:
9 rows, 18 query loads, query-reads accounted separately.  test-mh2lm-mdss-adt.py
proves the property needs the node (`--rsc-query` without `--rsc-channel` is a
ValueError), that it adds no node and no window, and that the gated clock trees
still carry no channel node at all.

No clocks, no votes, no writes: C3-02 moves a query, not a policy.  The display
owner remains the only voter and the only writer in this stage.

